<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augustus (Roman emperor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/63-bce">63 BCE</a> – 14 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Octavian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augustus, born Gaius Octavius and known as Octavian before 27 BCE, was the first Roman emperor. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Cleopatra at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a> in 31 BCE and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>. He then established the principate, reorganising the army, provinces and finances, and ruled until his death in 14 CE as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The league faded under Roman rule; after Actium in 31 BCE <b>Augustus</b> moved much of its population to his new city of Nicopolis.</blockquote><a href="/state/acarnanian-league">Acarnanian League</a></li><li><blockquote>In 27 BCE <b>Augustus</b> enlarged it as Hispania Tarraconensis, adding the north-west after the Cantabrian Wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a></li><li><blockquote>From 27 BCE <b>Augustus</b> reorganised it into Hispania Baetica and Lusitania, though the formal division took several years.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 14 BCE <b>Augustus</b> founded the colony of Caesaraugusta on the site of Salduie, and the Sedetani merged into Roman provincial society.</blockquote><a href="/state/sedetani">Sedetani</a></li><li><blockquote>In 26 BCE <b>Augustus</b> made Segisamo his camp for the campaign against the Cantabri, and the Turmodigi were integrated into Hispania Tarraconensis.</blockquote><a href="/state/turmodigi">Turmodigi</a></li></ul></section>
